  "prompt": "[system]\nYou generate executable, reliable VRP metaheuristics for evolutionary search.\n\n[user]\nSTRATEGY m1_component_replacement: Create a modified version of one parent by replacing exactly one major component while preserving the parent algorithm's overall identity. Possible components include initialization, candidate generation, scoring, selection, update, acceptance, refinement, restart logic, or effort allocation.\n\nUse the provided parents as the primary source of design changes.\n\nIncorporate, adapt, recombine, repair, or refine the listed parents.\n\nDo not ignore the parents or generate an unrelated solver from scratch.\n\nVRP objective: lower maximum route distance across trucks is better. Feasibility is mandatory.\n\nPARENTS:\nCandidate cand_000003: regret_insertion_solver\nStatus: valid; gap: 21.672\nThought: Regret-based construction prioritizing customers with high difference between best and second-best insertion cost, followed by local search (relocate and 2-opt) focusing on minimizing maximum route distance. Deterministic tie-breaking using best cost and index.\nMean gap: 21.67196571071025\nMean max route distance: 2.4938130227037023\nMean total route distance: 14.665760551243489\nGap by instance size: {'100': 21.67196571071025}\nGap by truck count: {'6': 21.67196571071025}\nCode:\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\ndef solve_vrp(distance_matrix, truck_count):\n    n = distance_matrix.shape[0]\n    depot = 0\n    customers = list(range(1, n))\n    unassigned = set(customers)\n    routes = [[depot, depot] for _ in range(truck_count)]\n    \n    # Helper to compute route distance\n    def route_dist(route):\n        d = 0\n        for i in range(len(route)-1):\n            d += distance_matrix[route[i], route[i+1]]\n        return d\n    \n    # Helper to compute insertion cost and best position for a customer in a route\n    def best_insertion(cust, route):\n        best_cost = float('inf')\n        best_pos = -1\n        # route has at least two entries (depot at start and end)\n        for pos in range(1, len(route)):\n            i = route[pos-1]\n            j = route[pos]\n            cost = distance_matrix[i, cust] + distance_matrix[cust, j] - distance_matrix[i, j]\n            if cost < best_cost:\n                best_cost = cost\n                best_pos = pos\n        return best_cost, best_pos\n    \n    # Regret insertion construction\n    while unassigned:\n        # Compute regret for each unassigned customer\n        best_regret = -1\n        best_cust = -1\n        best_route_idx = -1\n        best_pos = -1\n        best_cost_for_cust = float('inf')\n        \n        for cust in unassigned:\n            costs = []\n            for r_idx, route in enumerate(routes):\n                cost, pos = best_insertion(cust, route)\n                costs.append((cost, r_idx, pos))\n            # Sort by cost\n            costs.sort(key=lambda x: x[0])\n            if len(costs) == 1:\n                # Only one feasible route\n                regret = costs[0][0] * 2  # large regret\n            else:\n                regret = costs[1][0] - costs[0][0]\n            # Tie-breaking: if regret same, prefer higher best cost\n            if regret > best_regret or (regret == best_regret and costs[0][0] > best_cost_for_cust):\n                best_regret = regret\n                best_cust = cust\n                best_cost_for_cust = costs[0][0]\n                best_route_idx = costs[0][1]\n                best_pos = costs[0][2]\n            elif regret == best_regret and costs[0][0] == best_cost_for_cust:\n                # further tie: smaller customer index\n                if cust < best_cust:\n                    best_cust = cust\n                    best_route_idx = costs[0][1]\n                    best_pos = costs[0][2]\n        # Insert best customer\n        route = routes[best_route_idx]\n        route.insert(best_pos, best_cust)\n        unassigned.remove(best_cust)\n    \n    # Initial best routes\n    best_routes = [list(r) for r in routes]\n    best_max = max(route_dist(r) for r in best_routes)\n    \n    # Local search improvement\n    # Limit iterations\n    n_customers = n - 1\n    max_iters = 2 * n_customers\n    for _ in range(max_iters):\n        improved = False\n        # Inter-route relocate: try moving each customer to each other route\n        for r_idx, route in enumerate(routes):\n            if len(route) <= 3:  # only depot and possibly one customer, can't relocate from empty route\n                continue\n            # Consider each customer in this route (excluding depots)\n            customers_in_route = route[1:-1]\n            for cust in customers_in_route:\n                # Remove cust from current route\n                new_route = [x for x in route if x != cust]\n                # Evaluate insertion into other routes\n                for other_idx, other_route in enumerate(routes):\n                    if other_idx == r_idx:\n                        continue\n                    cost, pos = best_insertion(cust, other_route)\n                    # Create candidate routes\n                    candidate_routes = [list(r) for r in routes]\n                    candidate_routes[r_idx] = new_route\n                    other_new = list(other_route)\n                    other_new.insert(pos, cust)\n                    candidate_routes[other_idx] = other_new\n                    # Compute max distance\n                    dists = [route_dist(r) for r in candidate_routes]\n                    new_max = max(dists)\n                    if new_max < best_max:\n                        best_max = new_max\n                        routes = candidate_routes\n                        improved = True\n                        break  # restart iteration\n                if improved:\n                    break\n            if improved:\n                break\n        if not improved:\n            # Intra-route 2-opt for each route\n            for r_idx, route in enumerate(routes):\n                if len(route) <= 4:\n                    continue\n                # Try all 2-opt swaps (reverse segments)\n                n_nodes = len(route)\n                best_imp = False\n                for i in range(1, n_nodes-2):\n                    for j in range(i+1, n_nodes-1):\n                        new_route = route[:i] + route[i:j+1][::-1] + route[j+1:]\n                        if route_dist(new_route) < route_dist(route):\n                            routes[r_idx] = new_route\n                            best_imp = True\n                            break\n                    if best_imp:\n                        break\n                if best_imp:\n                    # recompute best_max\n                    dists = [route_dist(r) for r in routes]\n                    new_max = max(dists)\n                    if new_max < best_max:\n                        best_max = new_max\n                    improved = True\n                    break\n        if not improved:\n            break\n    \n    # Ensure exactly truck_count routes, each [0,0] if empty\n    result = []\n    for r in routes:\n        if len(r) <= 2:\n            result.append([0, 0])\n        else:\n            # Ensure starts and ends with 0\n            if r[0] != 0:\n                r.insert(0, 0)\n            if r[-1] != 0:\n                r.append(0)\n            result.append(r)\n    # If more routes than truck_count? should not happen\n    while len(result) < truck_count:\n        result.append([0, 0])\n    return result\n```\n\nImplement exactly this interface:\n\ndef solve_vrp(\n    distance_matrix: np.ndarray,  # (n, n), depot is node 0\n    truck_count: int,\n) -> list[list[int]]:\n\nRules:\n- VRP is a static multi-truck routing problem; the objective is to minimize the maximum route distance across trucks.\n- Return exactly truck_count routes.\n- Each route must start at depot node 0 and end at depot node 0.\n- Every customer node 1..n-1 must appear exactly once across all routes.\n- An unused truck route must be [0, 0].\n- Use only distance_matrix; do not assume coordinates, instance size, truck count, or dataset details.\n- Keep all search and improvement loops finite and bounded by instance size; never use open-ended loops.\n- Call report_best_vrp(routes) whenever you find a better complete feasible route set, especially before expensive improvement loops.\n- Do not read/write files, use network, spawn subprocesses, or call external solvers.\n- Allowed imports only: numpy, math, random, heapq, itertools, collections, time.\n- No module-level mutable globals; the solver may be called many times across instances.\n\nInternal check before final JSON: correct solve_vrp signature, exactly truck_count routes,\nevery route starts and ends at 0, every customer appears exactly once, empty trucks use [0, 0],\nfinite instance-size-bounded loops, deterministic tie handling, report_best_vrp used for\nincumbents, allowed imports only, no I/O/network/subprocesses.\n\nReturn one JSON object and nothing else:\n\n{\n  \"name\": \"short snake_case_or_title name\",\n  \"thought\": \"brief public summary of the construction, balancing, improvement, and tie-break logic\",\n  \"code\": \"complete Python code as a JSON string\"\n}\n\nNo Markdown, fences, or text outside JSON. The code string must define solve_vrp.",
